  <h1>Cookie &amp; Client-Side Storage Policy</h1>
  <p><em>Effective Date: October 2026 | Version 3.2 | Document Reference: POL-CK-2026-V3</em></p>

  <p>
    Welcome to the <strong>YouTube to MP4</strong> Cookie Policy (accessible via <code>https://ytubeconvert.site</code>, referred to throughout this document as "the Service", "Platform", "we", "us", or "our"). This policy provides transparent, precise technical documentation regarding how our web application utilizes HTTP cookies, browser local storage (<code>localStorage</code>), session storage (<code>sessionStorage</code>), and associated client-side data mechanisms.
  </p>
  <p>
    We believe that high-utility web tools should never rely on covert tracking or surreptitious surveillance. In compliance with international data privacy legislation—including the European Union ePrivacy Directive (Directive 2002/58/EC as amended), the General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR / Regulation EU 2016/679), the UK Data Protection Act, and the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA as amended by the CPRA)—this policy outlines exactly what identifiers are stored on your device, the legitimate purpose and duration of each token, and the exact steps you can take to control or disable them at any time.
  </p>

  <h2>1. What Are Cookies and Client-Side Storage Technologies?</h2>
  <p>
    Modern web browsers provide multiple technical mechanisms for websites to persist state information locally on a client device without continually transmitting state through URL query parameters. Understanding these distinctions ensures you have full visibility over how data is handled on your hardware:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>HTTP Cookies:</strong> Small text files (typically limited to 4 kilobytes per cookie) transmitted between the web server and your browser through standard HTTP request and response headers (<code>Set-Cookie</code>). Once placed, your browser returns these tokens with subsequent HTTP requests directed to the origin domain (or specified third-party ad networks). Cookies can be either "Session Cookies" (which are automatically destroyed the moment you terminate your web browser session) or "Persistent Cookies" (which remain stored on your hard drive until a predetermined expiration timestamp is reached or until you manually clear your browsing data).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Web Storage (<code>localStorage</code> &amp; <code>sessionStorage</code>):</strong> High-capacity client-side key-value storage mechanisms introduced in HTML5 (capable of storing 5 to 10 megabytes per domain). Unlike HTTP cookies, web storage items are never automatically appended to outgoing HTTP request headers sent to our web servers. They remain completely sandboxed inside your local browser client until JavaScript explicitly reads or deletes them. We utilize <code>localStorage</code> exclusively for client-side user preferences such as your Dark/Light visual theme choice.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Security &amp; Challenge Tokens (Cloudflare Turnstile):</strong> Ephemeral cryptographic tokens generated during automated bot mitigation challenges. These verify that an incoming request originates from an authentic human user navigating a genuine browser rather than an automated scraping script or malicious DDoS botnet.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. The Legal Basis for Cookie Processing</h2>
  <p>
    Under European Union and international data protection jurisprudence, cookies and storage technologies are categorized based on whether user consent is legally mandated prior to placement:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Strictly Necessary &amp; Security Exemption:</strong> Under Article 5(3) of the ePrivacy Directive and Section 1798.100 of the CCPA, technical storage strictly required to deliver an information society service explicitly requested by the end user does not require prior opt-in consent. This includes Cloudflare bot mitigation headers, rate limiting cookies, and transient CSRF protection tokens. Without these, our web servers could not securely transmit media streams or defend against denial-of-service disruptions.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Prior Explicit Consent for Advertising &amp; Tracking:</strong> Non-essential tracking mechanisms—specifically commercial advertising cookies delivered via Google AdSense and third-party advertising exchanges—are legally conditioned upon your affirmative, informed, and freely given consent. We enforce Google Consent Mode v2 to ensure that marketing and ad-personalization cookies remain strictly inactive by default until you interact with our consent interface.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Detailed Categorization of Storage Used on This Platform</h2>
  <p>
    We maintain a lean, performance-optimized software architecture. Unlike legacy web converters that burden user browsers with dozens of hidden tracking beacons, our inventory is divided into four clearly defined operational tiers:
  </p>

  <h3>Tier 1: Strictly Necessary &amp; Security Storage</h3>
  <p>
    These items are essential for the technical stability, operational security, and load-balanced routing of our cloud infrastructure. Because our service cannot safely function without them, these tokens cannot be deactivated through our on-site settings, though you may block them via your browser configuration:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare Bot Mitigation (<code>__cf_bm</code>):</strong> Issued by our global edge delivery partner (Cloudflare Inc.) to manage incoming HTTP traffic, distinguish legitimate human browsers from malicious automated botnets, and preserve server stability during high-volume traffic spikes. Duration: 30 minutes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare Turnstile Verification:</strong> An ephemeral verification challenge token created when completing bot challenges during rapid video stream extraction. This protects our backend conversion cluster from automated resource exhaustion. Duration: Ephemeral session.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Tier 2: Functional &amp; Preference Storage</h3>
  <p>
    Functional storage allows our web application to remember choices you make during your visit, saving you from having to reconfigure your personal settings on every page reload:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Visual Theme Preference (<code>theme</code> in <code>localStorage</code>):</strong> Remembers whether you selected Dark Mode (our default high-contrast theme) or Light Mode across page transitions. If this key is cleared, the platform automatically defaults to your operating system's system-level color preference (via the CSS <code>prefers-color-scheme</code> media query). Duration: Persistent until browser cache is cleared.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cookie Choice Record (<code>user_cookie_consent</code> in <code>localStorage</code>):</strong> Records whether you accepted or declined non-essential advertising cookies when the consent notification appeared. This prevents the banner from intrusively reappearing on every subsequent page you view. Duration: 365 days.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Tier 3: Performance &amp; Privacy-Preserving Analytics</h3>
  <p>
    We collect minimal, privacy-preserving aggregate telemetry at the cloud edge (Cloudflare Web Analytics) to monitor aggregate traffic patterns, regional bandwidth consumption, and conversion success rates. This analytics engine operates completely cookie-free: it does not place persistent tracking files on your device, does not fingerprint your hardware, and does not monitor your cross-site web browsing activities.
  </p>

  <h3>Tier 4: Advertising &amp; Google AdSense Cookies</h3>
  <p>
    To keep <strong>YouTube to MP4</strong> 100% free for all users worldwide without requiring paid subscriptions, paywalls, or user account registrations, we partner with Google AdSense to display contextual web advertisements.
  </p>
  <p>
    When you provide affirmative consent through our consent controls, Google and its certified vendor partners may place HTTP cookies (such as <code>__gads</code>, <code>__gpi</code>, and <code>IDE</code>) to measure advertising performance, prevent the same advertisement from repeatedly displaying to the same visitor, and serve ads tailored to your general browsing interests. If you reject non-essential cookies, Google AdSense is dynamically instructed via Google Consent Mode v2 to deliver non-personalized advertisements (NPA) that rely solely on page context rather than personal user profiles.
  </p>

